.TH X3DCTL 1 "2026" "x3dctl 1.4.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
x3dctl \- Deterministic workload policy controller for AMD X3D processors

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B x3dctl
.RI [ options ] " command " [ application ... ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
x3dctl is intentionally built as a small UNIX-style system utility:
explicit commands, plain text configuration, clear privilege boundaries,
and no background daemon.

It provides a lightweight interface for:

- Switching global X3D operating modes
- Applying CPU affinity to specific CCDs
- Setting process scheduling class
- Adjusting niceness
- Applying I/O priority
- Steering GPU IRQ affinity based on system mode

Privileged operations are delegated to a restricted helper binary executed via sudo.

.SH MODE MODEL

x3dctl defines system posture using two explicit global modes:

.TP
.B gaming
Switches X3D to cache-priority mode.

GPU IRQs are steered to the frequency CCD to isolate interrupt handling
from latency-sensitive 3D V-Cache workloads.

.TP
.B performance
Switches X3D to frequency-priority mode.

GPU IRQ affinity is set to the full CPU mask (all cores available).

Mode transitions are deterministic and command-driven.

.SH PROFILE MODEL

Applications are mapped to predefined workload profiles using:

.I /etc/x3dctl.conf

Format:

Key=Value
application=profile

key must match the executable basename passed to run.
Directory paths are ignored.
Shell aliases are not resolved.

.EX
steam
/usr/bin/steam
"./steam"
.EE

Everything above is supported as well as flatpak's containing spaces.

Supported profiles:

.TP
.B gaming
Cache CCD, nice -5, SCHED_OTHER, BE/0 I/O priority.

.TP
.B workstation
Frequency CCD, nice 5, SCHED_BATCH, BE/4 I/O priority. 

.TP
.B frequency
Frequency CCD, nice 0, SCHED_OTHER, BE/0 I/O priority.

.TP
.B unrestricted
No CPU affinity restriction. The process may run on all available cores.
Applies a slight priority bias (nice -1).

If no mapping exists, the default profile is "gaming".

.SH COMMANDS

.TP
.B run
command applies process-level policy only.
It does not change the current global X3D mode.

This allows multiple workloads to run concurrently with different
process policy while a separate global mode remains active.

Example:

.B x3dctl run steam

.TP
.B gaming
Switch global X3D mode to cache-priority and apply IRQ steering.

.TP
.B performance
Switch global X3D mode to frequency-priority and full mask IRQ distribution.

.TP
.B toggle
Switch global X3D mode to the opposite of the current mode.

.TP
.B status
Display current X3D operating mode, GPU IRQ steering state,
and irqbalance service status. 

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.BR -v ", " --verbose
Enable verbose logging of mode changes and affinity actions.

.TP
.BR -q ", " --quiet
Suppress informational output.

.TP
.BR --no-irq
Disable GPU IRQ steering and restore GPU IRQ affinity  
to the full mask for the current invocation.

Example:
.EX
x3dctl --no-irq run steam (disable manual steering and launch steam from profile
mapped in .conf)
.EE

.SH CONFIGURATION

Configuration file:

.I /etc/x3dctl.conf

Rules:

- Must be owned by root
- Must not be writable by group or others
- Lines beginning with '#' are ignored
- Whitespace around '=' is permitted
- Inline comments after values are supported

Example:
.EX
steam=gaming
blender=frequency
flatpak run com.obsproject.Studio = workstation (is also permitted)
.EE

The key must match the application's basename.
Profiles are enforced inside the privileged helper and cannot be defined dynamically.

.SH RUN BEHAVIOR

When an application is specified after run command:

.EX
x3dctl run steam
.EE

- Resolves the application basename
- Looks up the configured built-in profile (defaults to gaming if no match)
- Applies process policy to the current shell context
- Replaces the shell with the target application via exec

- This does not change the global X3D mode
- This allows multiple concurrent workloads to run with different process policies
- separate global mode remains active


.SH SECURITY

x3dctl enforces strict privilege separation.

The helper binary:

- Validates configuration ownership
- Restricts scheduler classes
- Prevents realtime scheduling
- Applies deterministic CPU and IRQ policy

x3dctl defines its own default IRQ baseline (full CPU mask).
It does not restore your original interrupt distribution.
IRQ affinity changes are runtime-only and are not persistent across system reboots.

For process-only policy application(Local install), capability-based helper execution
(e.g. CAP_SYS_NICE, CAP_SYS_ADMIN) may be used as an optional convenience path.
Global mode changes still use the normal privileged path as fall back.

Set no-password: (enable/disable)

.SH PASSWORD-LESS(Manual) 
.EX
sudo make pw-install
sudo make pw-uninstall
.EE

.SH PASSWORD-LESS(AUR) 
.EX
sudo setcap 'cap_sys_nice,cap_sys_admin=ep' /usr/bin/x3dctl-helper
sudo setcap -r /usr/bin/x3dctl-helper
.EE

.SH FILES

Packaging Install(AUR):

.TP
.I $pkgdir/usr/bin/x3dctl
Frontend command-line interface.

.TP
.I $pkgdir/usr/bin/x3dctl-helper
Privileged helper binary.

.TP
.I /etc/x3dctl.conf
Application-to-profile mapping file.
Profiles affect only launched processes and do not modify global system posture.

.TP
.I $pkgdir/etc/sudoers.d/x3dctl
Sudo policy for helper execution.
 
Local Manual Install: 

.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/x3dctl
CLI frontend

.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/x3dctl-helper
Privileged helper binary.

.TP
.I /etc/x3dctl.conf
Application-to-profile mapping file.
Profiles affect only launched processes and do not modify global system posture.

.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/x3dctl
Sudo policy

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
0
Success

.TP
1
Invalid usage or runtime failure

.SH DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

x3dctl prioritizes:

- Maintain a small, UNIX-oriented command surface
- Deterministic behavior
- Clear privilege separation
- Minimal runtime overhead
- Transparent configuration
- System stability

No background daemon or automatic workload detection is used.

